# Commands run by the launchd job com.hakios.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.metalprops).
#
# The first boot in which iOS's own daemons can reach our Metal device: boot-arg metal=1 opens
# IOAcceleratorES::setProperties, and metal_props puts MetalPluginName and MetalPluginClassName
# there. From that moment a process that asks Metal composes
# /System/Library/Extensions/RedwoodMetal.bundle, opens it with NSBundle and takes
# RedwoodMetalDevice -- all of Metal's own discovery.
#
# say_run is what puts a program's lines in the kernel's log: a job's standard output ends at
# /dev/console and is kept nowhere (src/say_run.c, kern.hakios.say).
#
# The wait is so that SpringBoard and backboardd are already up and drawing with the GL renderer
# before the properties appear: what this run measures is discovery with the system running, not
# the window server taking a different road at startup.
#
# metal_drv_test --sweep is the one form of the test meant for a boot with launchd alive: it asks
# Metal for the system device (no dlopen, no MTLAddDevice, so no second device to abort on) and
# counts, per class, the methods Metal's protocols require that the driver does not answer.
#
# metal_props show prints the path Metal would open and whether it is there, which is the one
# thing to look at first when a sweep finds no device.
#
# The last line takes the properties off again at once.
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 40
/redwood/bin/say_run /redwood/bin/metal_props show
/redwood/bin/say_run /redwood/bin/metal_props on
/redwood/bin/say_run /redwood/bin/metal_props show
/redwood/bin/say_run /redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --sweep
/redwood/bin/say_run /redwood/bin/metal_props off
